    {
      "id": "ugadi",
      "topic": "culture",
      "title": "Ugadi",
      "body": "Ugadi is the Telugu New Year, in March or April. Families eat Ugadi pachadi, a chutney with six tastes: sweet jaggery, sour tamarind, bitter neem flowers, salt, chilli heat and raw mango. Together they stand for the mix of experiences the new year will bring.",
      "tags": [
        "culture",
        "festivals"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sounds",
      "topic": "sounds",
      "title": "Sounds to watch",
      "body": "Telugu distinguishes short and long vowels and dental vs retroflex consonants (త / ట, ద / డ). Aspirated consonants exist mainly in loanwords; native words rely more on length and retroflexion.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "t": "తా · టా",
          "r": "tā · ṭā",
          "e": "dental t vs retroflex ṭ"
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        "pronunciation"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "politeness",
      "topic": "grammar",
      "title": "నువ్వు and మీరు",
      "body": "నువ్వు (nuvvu) is familiar “you”; మీరు (mīru) is polite or plural “you”. Verb endings change with the level: వెళ్తున్నావు vs వెళ్తున్నారు “you are going”.",
      "table": {
        "head": [
